CommonAPI: do not reset the runtime from a static destructor.

The deinitializer resets and deletes the runtime at exit, when static objects
it still refers to may already be destroyed -- a use-after-free during exit.
Leave the runtime to process teardown.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/src/CommonAPI/Runtime.cpp
+++ b/src/CommonAPI/Runtime.cpp
@@ -33,15 +33,25 @@
 
 #ifndef _WIN32
 DEINITIALIZER(RuntimeDeinit) {
-    if (theRuntimePtr__) {
-        // TODO: This mutex is causing a crash due to the changes introduced with 938f3d1. Since
-        // this "deinitializer" only runs on the main thread, no mutex should be needed. Leaving a
-        // comment pending a refactor.
-        // std::lock_guard<std::mutex> itsLock(getMutex__);
-        theRuntimePtr__->reset();
-        delete theRuntimePtr__;
-        theRuntimePtr__ = nullptr;
-    }
+    // TODO: This mutex is causing a crash due to the changes introduced with 938f3d1. Since
+    // this "deinitializer" only runs on the main thread, no mutex should be needed. Leaving a
+    // comment pending a refactor.
+    // std::lock_guard<std::mutex> itsLock(getMutex__);
+    //
+    // The Runtime is deliberately NOT destroyed here. ELF finalisers run per DSO
+    // and their relative order is not something either library controls, so a
+    // binding's own deinitializer -- CommonAPI::SomeIP::FactoryDeinit(), which
+    // calls Runtime::unregisterFactory() -- can still run after this point. That
+    // locks Runtime::factoriesMutex_, an instance member of the object destroyed
+    // here, i.e. a use-after-free. glibc's pthread_mutex_lock happens to tolerate
+    // the stale mutex; QNX returns EINVAL, libc++ turns that into a thrown
+    // std::system_error, and the process aborts at exit with
+    // "mutex lock failed: Invalid argument".
+    //
+    // Outliving the process is harmless for a singleton: the OS reclaims the
+    // memory at exit, and leaving it alive keeps every late unregisterFactory()
+    // call valid.
+    theRuntimePtr__ = nullptr;
 }
 #endif
 
